1
00:00:00,940 --> 00:00:04,600
A cheaper model is not a migration if it hides a bad slice.

2
00:00:04,630 --> 00:00:10,220
Casa Brisa, a fictional shop: sixty-three synthetic tickets, two scripted
providers.

3
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:20,650
The edge slice falls from one point zero to point six six six seven: thirty-
three points, on twenty-one tickets. The cap is five.

4
00:00:21,950 --> 00:00:25,930
Two gates fail: the edge slice, and the paired test.

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,260
Eight tickets fail only on the new provider, none only on the old.

6
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:37,490
Harmful tickets: zero. Cost falls from about seven cents to one and a half,
simulated, on [EST] prices.

7
00:00:38,830 --> 00:00:46,930
Other scripted profiles: close says go, flat needs a human, degraded says no-
go. The gate is not always red.

8
00:00:48,250 --> 00:00:57,260
The forced regression: a degraded provider. Fourteen harmful tickets, the gate
goes red, exit one. That is the expected result.

9
00:00:58,640 --> 00:01:05,870
Limits. The no-go is built into the fake provider, on purpose. It is not a gap
between two actual models.

10
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:09,470
Latency is simulated, prices are [EST].

11
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:18,850
The README's live self-compare, same model on both sides, fifty-five and
fifty-six of sixty-three, is noise, not evidence. Not rerun here.

12
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:25,640
Decide in hours: freeze the set, count tickets, and a named human approves any
go.

13
00:01:25,760 --> 00:01:30,090
Not covered: live shadow, a live canary, contract review.

14
00:01:30,150 --> 00:01:38,190
Savings are [EST], about seventy-nine percent on simulated prices. A cheaper
model that fails the hard tickets is not a migration.

